<h1>Introduction to Football (Soccer)</h1>

<p><strong>Football</strong>, commonly referred to as <strong>soccer</strong> in some countries, is the most popular sport in the world. In the UK and most of the world, it's simply called football. It’s a team sport played between two teams of 11 players with the objective of scoring more goals than the opponent.</p>

<div class="highlight">
  <p>Football is a game of passion, strategy, teamwork, and skill.</p>
</div>

<h2><span class="emoji">⚽</span>Basic Rules of Football</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The match is played in two halves of 45 minutes each, with a 15-minute halftime break.</li>
  <li>The ball is in play as long as it stays within the field boundaries.</li>
  <li>Only the goalkeeper can use their hands, and only within their penalty area.</li>
  <li>Fouls, offsides, and handballs are penalised with free kicks, penalties, or cards.</li>
</ul>

<h2><span class="emoji">🧍‍♂️</span>Player Positions</h2>
<p>Each team fields 11 players in different roles:</p>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Position</th>
    <th>Role</th>
    <th>Example</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Goalkeeper</strong></td>
    <td>Stops the ball from entering the goal</td>
    <td>Alisson (Liverpool)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Defenders</strong></td>
    <td>Protect the goal and stop attacks</td>
    <td>Ruben Dias (Manchester City)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Midfielders</strong></td>
    <td>Link defense and attack, control the game</td>
    <td>Kevin De Bruyne (Manchester City)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Forwards</strong></td>
    <td>Score goals and lead attacks</td>
    <td>Harry Kane (Bayern Munich, formerly Spurs)</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2><span class="emoji">🏟️</span>Famous Football Competitions</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Premier League</strong> – Top division of English football.</li>
  <li><strong>FA Cup</strong> – Oldest football competition in the world.</li>
  <li><strong>UEFA Champions League</strong> – Top clubs from across Europe compete.</li>
  <li><strong>FIFA World Cup</strong> – International teams every four years.</li>
</ul>

